{{right|''Maudsley'', Body and Will, p. 96.}}}}
{{smaller block|Many of these quotations were probably intended as nothing more than ''accommodations''. {{right|''Paley''.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b'') Adjustment of differences; reconciliation, as of parties in dispute.}}
{{smaller block|The conformity and analogy of which I speak . . . has a strong tendency to facilitate ''accommodation'', and to produce a generous oblivion of the rancour of their quarrels.
{{right|''Burke'', On a Regicide Peace, i.}}}}
{{smaller block|To come to terms of ''accommodation''. {{right|''Macaulay''.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''c'') Convenience; the supplying of a want; aid.}}
{{smaller block|St. James’s Church had recently been opened for the ''accommodation'' of the inhabitants of this new quarter.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., iii.}}}}
'''2.''' The state of being accommodated; fitness; state of adaptation: followed by ''to'', sometimes by ''with''.
{{smaller block|The organization of the body with ''accommodation to'' its functions. {{right|''Sir M. Hale'', Orig. of Mankind, p. 53.}}}}
{{smaller block|Socinus' main design . . . was to bring all the mysteries of Christianity to a full ''accommodation with'' the general notions of man’s reason. {{right|''South'', Works, V. iii.}}}}
'''3.''' Anything which supplies a want, as in respect of ease, refreshment, and the like; anything furnished for use; a convenience: chiefly applied to lodgings: as, ''accommodation'' for man and beast: often used in the plural.
{{smaller block|They probably thought of the coach with some contempt, as an ''accommodation'' for people who had not their own gigs. {{right|''George Eliot'', Felix Holt, iv.}}}}
{{smaller block|Outside of the larger cities on the Continent you can get as wretched ''accommodations'' as you could desire for an enemy. {{right|''T. B. Aldrich'', Ponkapog to Pesth, p. 65.}}}}

